IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `tfaia-domain`

§ `Section 22757.10 -- Short title`

-- src:11 | Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.10.
-- src:12 | This chapter shall be known as the Transparency in Frontier Artificial Intelligence Act.
-- Inert: the name of the chapter.

§ `Section 22757.11 -- Definitions`

-- src:15 | Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11.

§§ `(a), (b): affiliate, artificial intelligence model`

-- "Affiliate" is used only in (j); the developer states its revenue together with its
-- affiliates as one figure. Whether a system is an "artificial intelligence model" is
-- asked as a fact, because the definition is a description, not a test.
-- src:16 | For purposes of this chapter:
-- src:17 | (a) “Affiliate” means a person controlling, controlled by, or under common control with a specified person, directly or indirectly, through one or more intermediaries.
-- src:18 | (b) “Artificial intelligence model” means an engineered or machine-based system that varies in its level of autonomy and that can, for explicit or implicit objectives, infer from the input it receives how to generate outputs that can influence physical or virtual environments.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(b) (src:16-18)
GIVEN m IS A `me and my model`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the model is an AI model within the chapter` m MEANS
    m's `the model is an artificial intelligence model`

§§ `(f): foundation model`

-- src:34 | (f) “Foundation model” means an artificial intelligence model that is all of the following:
-- src:35 | (1) Trained on a broad data set.
-- src:36 | (2) Designed for generality of output.
-- src:37 | (3) Adaptable to a wide range of distinctive tasks.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(f) (src:34-37)
GIVEN m IS A `me and my model`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the model is a foundation model` m IF
        `the model is an AI model within the chapter` m
    AND m's `the model was trained on a broad data set`
    AND m's `the model is designed for generality of output`
    AND m's `the model is adaptable to a wide range of distinctive tasks`

§§ `(i): frontier model`

-- "greater than 10^26": exactly 10^26 operations is not enough (F2).
-- src:40 | (i) (1) “Frontier model” means a foundation model that was trained using a quantity of computing power greater than 10^26 integer or floating-point operations.
-- src:41 | (2) The quantity of computing power described in paragraph (1) shall include computing for the original training run and for any subsequent fine-tuning, reinforcement learning, or other material modifications the developer applies to a preceding foundation model.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(i)(1) (src:40-41)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the computing power threshold, in operations` MEANS 100000000000000000000000000

-- "was trained using": the operations already used, including later fine-tuning,
-- reinforcement learning and other material modifications (i)(2). A model whose
-- training has begun but has not yet used more than 10^26 operations is not yet a
-- frontier model, even if the developer intends to go past it (F3).
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(i) (src:40-41)
GIVEN m IS A `me and my model`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the model is a frontier model` m IF
        `the model is a foundation model` m
    AND m's `the operations used to train the model so far` GREATER THAN `the computing power threshold, in operations`

§§ `(h): frontier developer`

-- "has trained, or initiated the training of, a frontier model, with respect to which
-- the person has used, or intends to use, at least as much computing power ... as would
-- meet ... subdivision (i)". Read as: the developer has trained or begun training a
-- foundation model for which the operations it has used, or intends to use, are more
-- than 10^26 (F3). The developer states the largest such figure over all its
-- foundation models, so that a developer can be a frontier developer through a model
-- other than the one an incident involves. A developer whose model has already used
-- more than 10^26 operations is one in any case.
-- src:39 | (h) “Frontier developer” means a person who has trained, or initiated the training of, a frontier model, with respect to which the person has used, or intends to use, at least as much computing power to train the frontier model as would meet the technical specifications found in subdivision (i).
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(h) (src:39)
GIVEN m IS A `me and my model`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I am a frontier developer` m IF
       m's `the most operations I have used or intend to use to train any foundation model I have trained or begun to train` GREATER THAN `the computing power threshold, in operations`
    OR (    m's `I have trained, or initiated the training of, the model`
        AND `the model is a frontier model` m)

-- "its frontier models" (section 22757.13(c)(1)): a frontier model the developer
-- trained or began to train.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(h), (i) (src:39)
GIVEN m IS A `me and my model`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the model is a frontier model of mine` m IF
        m's `I have trained, or initiated the training of, the model`
    AND `the model is a frontier model` m

§§ `(j): large frontier developer`

-- "in excess of five hundred million dollars": exactly $500,000,000 is not enough (F2).
-- src:42 | (j) “Large frontier developer” means a frontier developer that together with its affiliates collectively had annual gross revenues in excess of five hundred million dollars ($500,000,000) in the preceding calendar year.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(j) (src:42)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the revenue threshold, in dollars` MEANS 500000000

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(a), (j) (src:42)
GIVEN m IS A `me and my model`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I am a large frontier developer` m IF
        `I am a frontier developer` m
    AND m's `the annual gross revenues of me and my affiliates in the preceding calendar year` GREATER THAN `the revenue threshold, in dollars`

§§ `(c) and section 22757.16: catastrophic risk`

-- A catastrophic risk is a RISK; kind 2 of critical safety incident is "harm resulting
-- from the materialization of" one. Read as: the event the risk describes has
-- happened -- a frontier model, doing one of (A) to (C), materially contributed to the
-- death of or serious injury to more than 50 people, or to more than $1,000,000,000
-- of damage to or loss of property, arising from a single incident -- and none of the
-- exclusions in (c)(2) applies (F5). Loss of value of equity is not property damage
-- (section 22757.16); the developer leaves it out of the dollar figure.
-- src:19 | (c) (1) “Catastrophic risk” means a foreseeable and material risk that a frontier developer’s development, storage, use, or deployment of a frontier model will materially contribute to the death of, or serious injury to, more than 50 people or more than one billion dollars ($1,000,000,000) in damage to, or loss of, property arising from a single incident involving a frontier model doing any of the following:
-- src:20 | (A) Providing expert-level assistance in the creation or release of a chemical, biological, radiological, or nuclear weapon.
-- src:21 | (B) Engaging in conduct with no meaningful human oversight, intervention, or supervision that is either a cyberattack or, if the conduct had been committed by a human, would constitute the crime of murder, assault, extortion, or theft, including theft by false pretense.
-- src:22 | (C) Evading the control of its frontier developer or user.
-- src:23 | (2) “Catastrophic risk” does not include a foreseeable and material risk from any of the following:
-- src:24 | (A) Information that a frontier model outputs if the information is otherwise publicly accessible in a substantially similar form from a source other than a foundation model.
-- src:25 | (B) Lawful activity of the federal government.
-- src:26 | (C) Harm caused by a frontier model in combination with other software if the frontier model did not materially contribute to the harm.
-- src:137 | The loss of value of equity does not count as damage to or loss of property for the purposes of this chapter.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(c), (l) and § 22757.16 (src:19-26) (src:137)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the people threshold` MEANS 50

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(c)(1) (src:19)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the property threshold, in dollars` MEANS 1000000000

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(c)(1)(A) to (C) (src:20-22)
GIVEN c IS A `what the model did`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the conduct is of a kind in (c)(1)` c MEANS
    CONSIDER c
    WHEN `none of the three kinds of conduct` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- The facts of the event, before asking whether the model is a frontier model.
-- (c)(2)(C), harm from the model in combination with other software where the model
-- did not materially contribute, is covered by the requirement in (c)(1) that the
-- model "materially contribute"; it needs no separate fact.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(c)(1), (c)(2) and § 22757.16 (src:19-26) (src:137)
GIVEN i IS A `the incident as I know it`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the facts fit the materialization of a catastrophic risk` i IF
        `the conduct is of a kind in (c)(1)` (i's `the model's conduct`)
    AND i's `the model materially contributed to the deaths, injuries, or damage`
    AND i's `the deaths, injuries, or damage arose from a single incident`
    AND (    i's `the number of people who died or were seriously injured` GREATER THAN `the people threshold`
         OR i's `the dollars of damage to, or loss of, property` GREATER THAN `the property threshold, in dollars`)
    AND (NOT i's `the harm came from information the model output that is otherwise publicly accessible in a substantially similar form from a source other than a foundation model`)
    AND (NOT i's `the harm came from lawful activity of the federal government`)

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(c) (src:19)
GIVEN m IS A `me and my model`, i IS A `the incident as I know it`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a catastrophic risk has materialized` m i IF
        `the model is a frontier model` m
    AND `the facts fit the materialization of a catastrophic risk` i

§§ `(d): critical safety incident`

-- Each kind names "a frontier model", so an event with a model that is not (yet) a
-- frontier model is no critical safety incident (F3). The kinds may overlap; every
-- kind that applies is listed (F6). The "facts fit" rules leave out the frontier-model
-- test, for section 22757.13(c)(4) (F8).
-- src:27 | (d) “Critical safety incident” means any of the following:
-- src:28 | (1) Unauthorized access to, modification of, or exfiltration of, the model weights of a frontier model that results in death or bodily injury.
-- src:29 | (2) Harm resulting from the materialization of a catastrophic risk.
-- src:30 | (3) Loss of control of a frontier model causing death or bodily injury.
-- src:31 | (4) A frontier model that uses deceptive techniques against the frontier developer to subvert the controls or monitoring of its frontier developer outside of the context of an evaluation designed to elicit this behavior and in a manner that demonstrates materially increased catastrophic risk.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(d), (d)(1) (src:27-31)
GIVEN i IS A `the incident as I know it`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the facts fit kind 1` i IF
        i's `there was unauthorized access to, modification of, or exfiltration of the model weights`
    AND i's `that access, modification, or exfiltration resulted in death or bodily injury`

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(d)(2) (src:29)
GIVEN i IS A `the incident as I know it`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the facts fit kind 2` i IF
    `the facts fit the materialization of a catastrophic risk` i

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(d)(3) (src:30)
GIVEN i IS A `the incident as I know it`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the facts fit kind 3` i IF
        i's `there was a loss of control of the model`
    AND i's `the loss of control caused death or bodily injury`

-- "outside of the context of an evaluation designed to elicit this behavior", and
-- "in a manner that demonstrates materially increased catastrophic risk", a judgment
-- the developer states as a fact.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(d)(4) (src:31)
GIVEN i IS A `the incident as I know it`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the facts fit kind 4` i IF
        i's `the model used deceptive techniques against me to subvert my controls or monitoring`
    AND (NOT i's `that happened in an evaluation designed to elicit that behavior`)
    AND i's `that behavior demonstrates materially increased catastrophic risk`

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(d) (src:27-31)
GIVEN k IS A `a kind of critical safety incident`, i IS A `the incident as I know it`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the facts fit the kind` k i MEANS
    CONSIDER k
    WHEN `kind 1, unauthorized access to, modification of, or exfiltration of model weights resulting in death or bodily injury` THEN `the facts fit kind 1` i
    WHEN `kind 2, harm resulting from the materialization of a catastrophic risk` THEN `the facts fit kind 2` i
    WHEN `kind 3, loss of control of a frontier model causing death or bodily injury` THEN `the facts fit kind 3` i
    WHEN `kind 4, deceptive subversion of the developer's controls or monitoring` THEN `the facts fit kind 4` i

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(d) (src:27-31)
GIVEN i IS A `the incident as I know it`
GIVETH A LIST OF `a kind of critical safety incident`
`the kinds the facts fit` i MEANS
    filter (GIVEN k YIELD `the facts fit the kind` k i)
           (LIST `kind 1, unauthorized access to, modification of, or exfiltration of model weights resulting in death or bodily injury`
               , `kind 2, harm resulting from the materialization of a catastrophic risk`
               , `kind 3, loss of control of a frontier model causing death or bodily injury`
               , `kind 4, deceptive subversion of the developer's controls or monitoring`)

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(d) (src:27-31)
GIVEN m IS A `me and my model`, i IS A `the incident as I know it`
GIVETH A LIST OF `a kind of critical safety incident`
`the kinds of critical safety incident that apply` m i MEANS
    IF `the model is a frontier model` m
    THEN `the kinds the facts fit` i
    ELSE LIST

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11(d) (src:27-31)
GIVEN m IS A `me and my model`, i IS A `the incident as I know it`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is a critical safety incident` m i IF
        `the model is a frontier model` m
    AND (   `the facts fit kind 1` i
         OR `the facts fit kind 2` i
         OR `the facts fit kind 3` i
         OR `the facts fit kind 4` i)

§§ `(e), (g), (k), (l): deploy, frontier AI framework, model weight, property`

-- Inert for reporting. Nothing in section 22757.13 turns on whether the model has been
-- deployed: the duty is for any critical safety incident "pertaining to one or more of
-- its frontier models", and the report says whether the incident "was associated with
-- internal use of a frontier model" (F7). "Model weight" and "property" are glosses
-- on kinds 1 and 2.
-- src:32 | (e) (1) “Deploy” means to make a frontier model available to a third party for use, modification, copying, or combination with other software.
-- src:33 | (2) “Deploy” does not include making a frontier model available to a third party for the primary purpose of developing or evaluating the frontier model.
-- src:38 | (g) “Frontier AI framework” means documented technical and organizational protocols to manage, assess, and mitigate catastrophic risks.
-- src:43 | (k) “Model weight” means a numerical parameter in a frontier model that is adjusted through training and that helps determine how inputs are transformed into outputs.
-- src:44 | (l) “Property” means tangible or intangible property.
